      "portalId": "095f9b9a-9e62-459c-9518-8728bb3f2912",
      "title": "Lesson 18 | Ṣarīḥ/Kināyah /Types of Words in Terms of Istidlāl",
      "overview": "In this lesson, we delved into the intricate concepts of \"Haqiqa\" and \"Majaz,\" exploring their applications and how they influence the understanding of \"Ahkaam\" (rulings). We discussed the significance of \"Huroof al-Ma'ani\" and their varied meanings, which can impact the interpretation of legal texts. Moving forward, we transitioned into the categories of \"Sareeh\" and \"Kinaya,\" emphasizing that these are not in opposition to \"Haqiqa\" and \"Majaz,\" but rather different dimensions of word usage. We highlighted the distinction between explicit and implicit meanings, where \"Sareeh\" refers to words with clear, apparent meanings, while \"Kinaya\" involves meanings that require contextual clues or \"Qareena\" to be understood.\n\nFurthermore, we examined the role of intention in legal judgments, particularly in the context of \"Qadha\" and \"Diyana.\" We explored how judgments are often based on the apparent words and actions, leaving the inner intentions to be judged by Allah. This led us to discuss the Hanafi distinction between these two concepts, illustrating how words can be interpreted differently based on their usage and context. We concluded by touching upon the importance of understanding the listener's perspective in extracting meanings from words, setting the stage for our next discussion on \"Bayan\" and its relevance in Hanafi jurisprudence.",
